Thanks to Christian Keller, Jean Adams, Zhaorong(Jo) JIAO, Patrick
Connolly,Bert Gunter, Joe Verducci and Z. Todd Taylor for all your help.    
        
        Christian's solution is simple and elegant:               

X <- data.frame(Rank1=c("A","A","B"),
                Rank2=c("B","B","C"),
                Rank3=c("C","C","A"))

X

  Rank1 Rank2 Rank3 
1     A     B     C
2     A     B     C
3     B     C     A

foo <- function(x){
  x <- factor(x, levels=c("A","B","C"))
  table(x)
}

data.frame(NoteName=c("A","B","C"), sapply(X, FUN=foo))

  NoteName Rank1 Rank2 Rank3 
1        A     2     0     1
2        B     1     2     0
3        C     0     1     2

(Note:In x <- factor(x, levels=c("A","B","C")), the levels argument is very
import here because "A","B" and "C" do not appear in each columns all the
time.  If "A","B" and "C" appear in each colum all the time,  a simple line
such as, date.frame(apply(X,2,summary.factor (or table))) would do the work.

        Jean Adams's is a very nice one too:

sur <- sort(unique(as.matrix(X[, 2:3])))
     Y <- as.data.frame(cbind(sur, t(sapply(sur, function(r) 
          apply(X[, 2:3]==r, 2, sum)))))
     names(Y) <- c("NoteName", paste("Rank", 1:3, sep=""))
     Y

